{"abstract":"Although a plethora of tools are available for Global Software Engineering (GSE) teams, it is being realized increasingly that the most prevalent desktop metaphor underpinning the majority of tools have several inherent limitations. We have proposed that Activity-Based Computing (ABC) can be a promising alternative to build tools for GSE. However, significant effort is required to introduce a new paradigm; there is a need of sound theoretical foundation based on activity theory to address challenges faced by tools in GSE. This paper reports our effort aimed at building theoretical foundations for applying activity theory to GSE. We analyze and explain the fundamental concepts of activity theory, and how they can be applied by using examples of software architecture design and evaluation processes. We describe the kind of data model and architectural support required for applying activity theory in building supporting infrastructure for GSE, and describe a proof of concept prototype.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Tell, Paolo","Ali Babar, Muhammad"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382471"],"event":{"end":"2012-08-30","place":"Porto Alegre, br","start":"2012-08-27","title":"International Conference on Global Software Engineering","year":"2012"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Activity theory","Global Software Engineering"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"activity theory applied to global software engineering theoretical foundations and implications for tool builders","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84870696387","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382471","identifier":[{"id":"84870696387","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-50714ee6-03f1-4736-ae25-9bba5ee17e93"],"keyword":["Activity Theory","Global Software Engineering"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A02465D5AF5FC8479D98502D205671B4"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Software Engineering, Software Engineering","The Department, Software & Systems"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"tellpau","last":"Tell","locid":"2b8ff3a4-e9b0-403d-8bd8-1bf26121c234","name":"Tell, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0001-7391-0046","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Muhammad","key":"alibabarpau","last":"Ali Babar","name":"Ali Babar, Muhammad","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9781467323574"],"pages":"21-30","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"Global Software Engineering (ICGSE), 2012 IEEE Seventh International Conference on"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84870696387"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-50714ee6-03f1-4736-ae25-9bba5ee17e93"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Activity Theory applied to Global Software Engineering: Theoretical Foundations and Implications for Tool Builders","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382471","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
{"abstract":"Context: A wide variety of technologies have been developed to support Global Software Development (GSD). However, the information about the dozens of available solutions is quite diverse and scattered making it quite difficult to have an overview able to identify common trends and unveil research gaps. Objective: The objective of this research is to systematically identify and classify a comprehensive list of the technologies that have been developed and/or used for supporting GSD teams. Method: This study has been undertaken as a Systematic Mapping Study (SMS). Our searches identified 1958 papers out of which 182 were found suitable for inclusion in this study. Results: We have identified 412 technologies reported to be developed and/or used for supporting GSD teams from 182 papers. The identified technologies have been analyzed in order to categorize them using four main classification schemas for providing a framework that can help identify the categories that have attracted significant amount of research and commercial efforts, and the research areas where there are gaps to be filled. Conclusions: The findings show that whilst commercial and open source solutions are predominantly focused on specialized tools as well as platforms, research effort was concentrated on providing integrated environments, frameworks, and plug-in based solutions. Considering the findings in the context of previously proposed research agendas, some of the key challenges for GSD research (i.e., collaborative tools and innovative knowledge management systems) shows that lots of collaborative technologies have been reported, but knowledge management is being addressed by focusing on supporting awareness, which is being considered as important as the three elements of 3C model (i.e., communication, collaboration, and coordination). We also conclude that future effort in this area should pay more attention to devising solutions which can fulfill several kinds of requirements necessitated by a broader set of challenges being faced by GSD practitioners rather than tackling individual issues.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Tell, Paolo","Ali Babar, Muhammad"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384579"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Collaborative Tools","Global Software Development","Knowledge management","Research Gaps","Systematic mapping study"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a systematic mapping study of tools for distributed software development teams","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/39496798/ITUTR2012161pdf.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-384579","ids":["itu-e1094ec3-29dd-4140-960b-f6c06f2b3559"],"keyword":["Collaborative Tools","Global Software Development","Knowledge Management","Research Gaps","Systematic Mapping Study"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-F555DBF402691B44E0BCA5694369604B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/39496798/ITUTR2012161pdf.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Software Engineering, Software Engineering","The Department, Software & Systems"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"tellpau","last":"Tell","locid":"2b8ff3a4-e9b0-403d-8bd8-1bf26121c234","name":"Tell, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0001-7391-0046","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Muhammad","key":"alibabarpau","last":"Ali Babar","name":"Ali Babar, Muhammad","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["16006100"],"issue":"TR-2012-161","pages":"107","place":"Copenhagen, Denmark","publisher":"IT-Universitetet i København","repno":"IT University Technical Report Series, TR-2012-161","series":"IT University Technical Report Series","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e1094ec3-29dd-4140-960b-f6c06f2b3559"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A Systematic Mapping Study of Tools for Distributed Software Development Teams","type":"Report","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384579","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
{"abstract":"We conducted a two-stage genome-wide association study of renal cell carcinoma (RCC) in 3,772 affected individuals (cases) and 8,505 controls of European background from 11 studies and followed up 6 SNPs in 3 replication studies of 2,198 cases and 4,918 controls. Two loci on the regions of 2p21 and 11q13.3 were associated with RCC susceptibility below genome-wide significance. Two correlated variants (r(2) = 0.99 in controls), rs11894252 (P = 1.8 × 10(-8)) and rs7579899 (P = 2.3 × 10(-9)), map to EPAS1 on 2p21, which encodes hypoxia-inducible-factor-2 alpha, a transcription factor previously implicated in RCC. The second locus, rs7105934, at 11q13.3, contains no characterized genes (P = 7.8 × 10(-14)). In addition, we observed a promising association on 12q24.31 for rs4765623, which maps to SCARB1, the scavenger receptor class B, member 1 gene (P = 2.6 × 10(-8)). Our study reports previously unidentified genomic regions associated with RCC risk that may lead to new etiological insights.","author_count":104,"contributors":["Purdue, Mark P","Johansson, Mattias","Zelenika, Diana","Toro, Jorge R","Scelo, Ghislaine","Moore, Lee E","Prokhortchouk, Egor","Wu, Xifeng","Kiemeney, Lambertus A","Gaborieau, Valerie","Jacobs, Kevin B","Chow, Wong-Ho","Zaridze, David","Matveev, Vsevolod","Lubinski, Jan","Trubicka, Joanna","Szeszenia-Dabrowska, Neonila","Lissowska, Jolanta","Rudnai, Péter","Fabianova, Eleonora","Bucur, Alexandru","Bencko, Vladimir","Foretova, Lenka","Janout, Vladimir","Boffetta, Paolo","Colt, Joanne S","Davis, Faith G","Schwartz, Kendra L","Banks, Rosamonde E","Selby, Peter J","Harnden, Patricia","Berg, Christine D","Hsing, Ann W","Grubb, Robert L","Boeing, Heiner","Vineis, Paolo","Clavel-Chapelon, Françoise","Palli, Domenico","Tumino, Rosario","Krogh, Vittorio","Panico, Salvatore","Duell, Eric J","Quirós, José Ramón","Sanchez, Maria-José","Navarro, Carmen","Ardanaz, Eva","Dorronsoro, Miren","Khaw, Kay-Tee","Allen, Naomi E","Bueno-de-Mesquita, H Bas","Peeters, Petra H M","Trichopoulos, Dimitrios","Linseisen, Jakob","Ljungberg, Börje","Overvad, Kim","Tjønneland, Anne","Romieu, Isabelle","Riboli, Elio","Mukeria, Anush","Shangina, Oxana","Stevens, Victoria L","Thun, Michael J","Diver, W Ryan","Gapstur, Susan M","Pharoah, Paul D","Easton, Douglas F","Albanes, Demetrius","Weinstein, Stephanie J","Virtamo, Jarmo","Vatten, Lars","Hveem, Kristian","Njølstad, Inger","Tell, Grethe S","Stoltenberg, Camilla","Kumar, Rajiv","Koppova, Kvetoslava","Cussenot, Olivier","Benhamou, Simone","Oosterwijk, Egbert","Vermeulen, Sita H","Aben, Katja K H","van der Marel, Saskia L","Ye, Yuanqing","Wood, Christopher G","Pu, Xia","Mazur, Alexander M","Boulygina, Eugenia S","Chekanov, Nikolai N","Foglio, Mario","Lechner, Doris","Gut, Ivo","Heath, Simon","Blanche, Hélène","Hutchinson, Amy","Thomas, Gilles","Wang, Zhaoming","Yeager, Meredith","Fraumeni, Joseph F","Skryabin, Konstantin G","McKay, James D","Rothman, Nathaniel","Chanock, Stephen J","Lathrop, Mark","Brennan, Paul"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-229260"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"51-200 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"genome wide association study of renal cell carcinoma identifies two susceptibility loci on 000000000002p000000000021 and 000000000011q000000000013 000000000003","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/ng.723","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-229260","identifier":[{"id":"PMC3049257","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"21131975","type":"pmid"},{"id":"78651257523","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-af30f47c-1aba-41fc-ba3a-dffbec645b03"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-52F590E39A18EF